1
00:00:00,366 --> 00:00:04,033
An online store found the best way to make people spend more isn't discounting

2
00:00:04,266 --> 00:00:06,333
It's buy a little more and shipping is free

3
00:00:06,566 --> 00:00:11,966
It never cut the price of the products yet customers stuffed their carts fuller all on their own

4
00:00:12,366 --> 00:00:15,033
This is 100 Economic Principles from Pixel Breath

5
00:00:15,033 --> 00:00:17,100
Number 3 Marginal Analysis

6
00:00:17,433 --> 00:00:20,766
No jargon just one everyday calculation to make it clear

7
00:00:20,966 --> 00:00:29,900
Years ago Harvard Business School researcher Donald Ngwe and colleagues studied order data from Zalora a fashion e-commerce site in the Philippines

8
00:00:30,066 --> 00:00:32,433
They weren't looking at a few hundred survey answers

9
00:00:32,533 --> 00:00:36,666
But over two million real orders from 2012 to 2016

10
00:00:36,666 --> 00:00:39,200
The store had tested different shipping rules

11
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:41,233
Sometimes shipping was simply free

12
00:00:41,233 --> 00:00:45,233
Sometimes customers were told orders had to hit a threshold first

13
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:48,033
The study surfaced a fascinating result

14
00:00:48,033 --> 00:00:53,166
When free shipping required a minimum customers spent 9.4% more per order on average

15
00:00:53,433 --> 00:00:56,466
But their total number of purchases dropped by 6.4%

16
00:00:56,466 --> 00:00:58,800
This isn't a story about how promotions are evil

17
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:01,466
Because some of the extra things people bought they truly needed

18
00:01:01,533 --> 00:01:04,500
Stocking up early on paper towels and detergent can be a great deal

19
00:01:04,533 --> 00:01:08,433
What's really worth watching is how that threshold shifted people's attention

20
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:11,466
At first you face a six-yuan delivery fee

21
00:01:11,466 --> 00:01:12,400
A clear question

22
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:15,233
Are you willing to pay six yuan to get something delivered

23
00:01:15,233 --> 00:01:17,866
But once the page says buy a little more for free shipping

24
00:01:17,866 --> 00:01:20,000
The question in your head quietly changes

25
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,400
You stop asking do I need this item

26
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:25,300
You start asking how far am I from free shipping

27
00:01:25,466 --> 00:01:27,700
The first question judges the product

28
00:01:27,700 --> 00:01:30,900
The second chases a progress bar that's already lit up

29
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:33,000
These two sound alike

30
00:01:33,233 --> 00:01:35,066
But they're not the same calculation

31
00:01:35,066 --> 00:01:36,700
The store doesn't need to force you to buy

32
00:01:37,066 --> 00:01:39,666
It just puts almost there in front of your eyes

33
00:01:39,666 --> 00:01:42,500
The rest you complete for your cart yourself

34
00:01:42,666 --> 00:01:45,800
That's also why many people feel nothing when the package arrives

35
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:50,633
Only after opening it do they find one item they can't remember why they added

36
00:01:50,633 --> 00:01:52,700
It was never an independent choice

37
00:01:52,700 --> 00:01:55,133
It just got pulled in by everything before it

38
00:01:55,133 --> 00:01:57,900
What marginal analysis unpacks is exactly this kind of add-on

39
00:01:57,933 --> 00:02:00,900
It doesn't rush to ask whether the whole cart is worth it

40
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:04,000
It zooms in on the very last item added

41
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,533
What new benefit did this one actually bring

42
00:02:06,533 --> 00:02:08,566
And what new cost did it take away

43
00:02:08,633 --> 00:02:10,566
If you don't recalculate these two sides

44
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:13,466
The smartest promotions don't make things feel cheap

45
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:16,666
They make you forget you're making a second decision

46
00:02:16,666 --> 00:02:19,200
Marginal sounds like a textbook word

47
00:02:19,333 --> 00:02:21,200
In plain language it just means change

48
00:02:21,400 --> 00:02:24,433
You've already bought some done some invested some

49
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:27,166
What happens if you add a little more now

50
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:31,666
Economics doesn't first ask whether the whole eighty-yuan cart is worth it

51
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:35,200
It asks whether the item you just added is worth it

52
00:02:35,466 --> 00:02:38,966
Say your cart already holds 72 yuan of necessities

53
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:40,466
Delivery costs six

54
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:43,566
Checking out as planned you pay 78 in total

55
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:46,900
The page says add eight more for free shipping

56
00:02:47,066 --> 00:02:49,800
You add a small thing you never planned to buy

57
00:02:50,033 --> 00:02:51,600
In the end you pay 80

58
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:55,566
Many would say I spent eight to save six so I'm not losing

59
00:02:55,733 --> 00:02:58,300
But that sentence blends two calculations into one

60
00:02:58,433 --> 00:03:00,166
The original plan was 78

61
00:03:00,266 --> 00:03:01,833
The new plan is 80

62
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,400
Your real additional cash outlay is two yuan

63
00:03:05,666 --> 00:03:07,566
Then comes the more important question

64
00:03:08,033 --> 00:03:10,366
Is that new item worth those two yuan

65
00:03:10,666 --> 00:03:12,666
If you were going to buy it next week anyway

66
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:16,500
If it saves you the hassle of placing a separate order later

67
00:03:16,666 --> 00:03:19,800
Then its added benefit may be greater than two yuan

68
00:03:20,300 --> 00:03:21,833
This add-on is fine

69
00:03:22,133 --> 00:03:24,600
But if it sits in a drawer for a year

70
00:03:24,666 --> 00:03:26,800
You paid more than just two yuan

71
00:03:27,033 --> 00:03:32,100
There's also the space it takes the hassle of tidying later and the illusion that you already owned it

72
00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:34,166
That is marginal cost

73
00:03:34,333 --> 00:03:36,300
It's not just a price tag

74
00:03:36,333 --> 00:03:41,466
It also includes the added time space energy and tomorrow's choices it takes away

75
00:03:41,733 --> 00:03:44,633
And marginal benefit isn't just a discount number either

76
00:03:44,733 --> 00:03:48,233
It's what this addition actually solves for you

77
00:03:48,466 --> 00:03:50,600
The simplest rule in economics is

78
00:03:50,633 --> 00:03:53,766
If added benefit exceeds added cost keep going

79
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,966
If added benefit is less than added cost pause

80
00:03:57,100 --> 00:03:59,233
Note this isn't about less is always better

81
00:03:59,333 --> 00:04:02,466
Nor is it about turning all your joy into a spreadsheet

82
00:04:02,733 --> 00:04:04,766
Some additions are absolutely worth it

83
00:04:05,100 --> 00:04:06,666
Rainy day and no medicine at home

84
00:04:06,700 --> 00:04:09,333
You pay a little more delivery for medicine in time

85
00:04:09,333 --> 00:04:11,033
That added benefit is huge

86
00:04:11,266 --> 00:04:14,633
Tomorrow's presentation is missing one key number

87
00:04:14,633 --> 00:04:16,700
You spend twenty extra minutes verifying it

88
00:04:16,700 --> 00:04:18,400
Those twenty minutes are worth it too

89
00:04:18,533 --> 00:04:21,166
Marginal analysis never tells you not to add

90
00:04:21,266 --> 00:04:25,600
It just won't let the first round's reasons automatically vouch for the last one

91
00:04:25,633 --> 00:04:27,500
This differs from sunk cost

92
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:31,700
Sunk cost reminds you that money already spent shouldn't chain down your next step

93
00:04:31,700 --> 00:04:32,266
Marginal analysis goes one step further

94
00:04:32,266 --> 00:04:36,200
It asks whether the next step itself brings you enough new value

95
00:04:36,700 --> 00:04:39,400
It's also not quite the same as diminishing marginal utility

96
00:04:39,433 --> 00:04:43,966
Diminishing utility asks why the last sips of milk tea satisfy less than the first

97
00:04:44,100 --> 00:04:46,233
That's the story for Number 5

98
00:04:46,266 --> 00:04:47,466
This one is broader

99
00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:51,633
Whether added benefits shrink or added costs grow

100
00:04:51,700 --> 00:04:54,000
Once the calculation for this last step flips

101
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,000
You have a reason to stop and recalculate

102
00:04:56,133 --> 00:04:58,633
The last item in your cart is easy to see

103
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:01,700
The last hour of your time is much easier to miss

104
00:05:01,733 --> 00:05:04,500
At seven pm you find the deck is still one page short

105
00:05:04,500 --> 00:05:06,700
You spend another half hour fixing the logic

106
00:05:06,700 --> 00:05:09,000
The next day's meeting goes far more smoothly

107
00:05:09,100 --> 00:05:10,300
That half hour was worth it

108
00:05:10,300 --> 00:05:12,966
At eleven pm you open version seven again

109
00:05:13,133 --> 00:05:14,600
This time nothing is missing

110
00:05:14,633 --> 00:05:17,833
You just want to reword the title on every page

111
00:05:18,033 --> 00:05:19,866
The slides might look a bit tidier

112
00:05:19,900 --> 00:05:22,900
But what you're trading away is no ordinary half hour

113
00:05:22,900 --> 00:05:23,900
It's sleep

114
00:05:24,533 --> 00:05:26,700
It's patience for tomorrow's numbers

115
00:05:27,033 --> 00:05:29,633
And it's the silly mistakes that show up the next day

116
00:05:29,700 --> 00:05:30,933
Same thirty minutes

117
00:05:30,933 --> 00:05:33,466
The earlier one closed a critical gap

118
00:05:33,466 --> 00:05:37,366
The later one may just be giving your anxiety a reason to keep working

119
00:05:37,466 --> 00:05:41,633
Economist John Pencavel studied output and working hours in munitions factories

120
00:05:41,900 --> 00:05:43,466
His finding was measured

121
00:05:43,666 --> 00:05:47,600
At lower ranges output rose roughly along with hours

122
00:05:47,633 --> 00:05:52,266
But past a threshold output could still rise yet slower and slower

123
00:05:52,433 --> 00:05:55,866
The sample was factory workers from one specific historical period

124
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:00,066
It can't declare which overtime hour stops paying off for everyone

125
00:06:00,200 --> 00:06:02,400
But it makes one thing very clear

126
00:06:02,633 --> 00:06:05,500
Time is not a ruler with equal-length markings

127
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:08,366
Once stamina attention and sleep start running out

128
00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:11,633
The cost of that final added hour changes

129
00:06:11,833 --> 00:06:13,400
It's no longer just one hour

130
00:06:13,466 --> 00:06:16,166
It also borrows part of tomorrow's judgment

131
00:06:16,333 --> 00:06:17,700
Learning works the same way

132
00:06:17,833 --> 00:06:21,900
The first twenty minutes of research may finally show you where the problem is

133
00:06:22,100 --> 00:06:25,500
Another half hour might find a case you can actually use

134
00:06:25,633 --> 00:06:29,833
But by hour three you're opening the seventh article on the same point

135
00:06:30,133 --> 00:06:32,633
The added material doesn't make you better at judging

136
00:06:32,666 --> 00:06:35,400
It just lets you keep not writing the first sentence

137
00:06:35,400 --> 00:06:38,066
AI tools speed this whole process up

138
00:06:38,333 --> 00:06:40,000
First you ask it for ten directions

139
00:06:40,033 --> 00:06:42,866
You might see two paths you never thought of

140
00:06:43,033 --> 00:06:44,966
Then you have it rewrite the title twenty times

141
00:06:45,133 --> 00:06:47,066
The options look more plentiful

142
00:06:47,133 --> 00:06:49,966
But the step where you actually decide keeps getting pushed later

143
00:06:50,300 --> 00:06:51,566
The tool isn't the problem

144
00:06:51,866 --> 00:06:56,400
The problem is whether the next round you add actually moves you toward done

145
00:06:56,733 --> 00:06:57,766
If not

146
00:06:57,833 --> 00:07:01,400
That round of new answers may already have very low marginal benefit

147
00:07:01,533 --> 00:07:05,366
And its marginal cost is the evening you spent delaying the decision

148
00:07:05,600 --> 00:07:08,300
So marginal analysis isn't against effort

149
00:07:08,333 --> 00:07:12,700
It's against smuggling the first hour's value into the last hour

150
00:07:13,100 --> 00:07:17,000
Its most practical use isn't making you carry a calculator around

151
00:07:17,266 --> 00:07:20,233
It just asks you to pause ten seconds before adding more

152
00:07:20,533 --> 00:07:22,966
Don't first ask how much I've already spent

153
00:07:23,100 --> 00:07:25,900
First ask what will the next one actually give me

154
00:07:26,266 --> 00:07:29,466
Then ask what else will it take from me beyond the price

155
00:07:29,633 --> 00:07:34,800
Finally ask if the earlier ones didn't exist would I still do this step on its own

156
00:07:35,033 --> 00:07:36,666
When you're about to pad an order for free shipping

157
00:07:36,666 --> 00:07:38,233
Don't stare at how much is still short

158
00:07:38,233 --> 00:07:41,266
First ask was this item already on my list

159
00:07:41,266 --> 00:07:43,233
When you're about to work one more hour

160
00:07:43,266 --> 00:07:45,266
Don't say I've already come this far

161
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:49,166
First ask will this hour solve tomorrow morning's biggest problem

162
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:51,100
When you're about to read one more article

163
00:07:51,100 --> 00:07:53,433
Don't say I've already researched this long

164
00:07:53,733 --> 00:07:58,166
First ask does it bring me closer to delivering or just make me start later

165
00:07:58,333 --> 00:08:00,866
These questions won't turn life into penny-pinching

166
00:08:00,900 --> 00:08:04,300
They just take each addition back out of autopilot

167
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:06,233
Back to that e-commerce study

168
00:08:06,433 --> 00:08:08,466
Free shipping over a threshold isn't wrong in itself

169
00:08:08,533 --> 00:08:11,266
Sometimes it helps the store cover logistics costs

170
00:08:11,333 --> 00:08:14,366
And sometimes it lets you buy what you truly need a bit earlier

171
00:08:14,733 --> 00:08:19,233
The only issue is the page won't judge that added item's value for you

172
00:08:19,266 --> 00:08:22,100
It only reminds you how far you are from the threshold

173
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:27,433
What can truly judge for you is still the money time and attention you have left

174
00:08:27,800 --> 00:08:31,433
You also don't need to turn every add-on into a trial of yourself

175
00:08:31,700 --> 00:08:34,300
Some things really are easier bought early

176
00:08:34,333 --> 00:08:37,800
Some final hours really do save tomorrow's trouble

177
00:08:37,900 --> 00:08:41,033
Marginal analysis doesn't demand you always refuse additions

178
00:08:41,200 --> 00:08:46,666
It just wants to take the wheel back from the glowing threshold and return it to your real needs

179
00:08:46,933 --> 00:08:50,700
Because what people regret most usually isn't spending a large sum

180
00:08:50,933 --> 00:08:55,833
It's knowing that addition has no new value yet feeling it's a pity to let it go

181
00:08:56,100 --> 00:08:59,433
Next time you see buy a little more for free shipping

182
00:08:59,500 --> 00:09:01,600
Try translating it into another sentence

183
00:09:01,733 --> 00:09:03,433
Not I'm just short of a deal

184
00:09:03,433 --> 00:09:05,833
But I'm about to make a new choice

185
00:09:06,033 --> 00:09:09,400
If that added item truly gives more than it takes

186
00:09:09,466 --> 00:09:10,266
Buy it

187
00:09:10,500 --> 00:09:13,500
If all you can answer is well I've come this far anyway

188
00:09:13,500 --> 00:09:16,033
Then stop finding old reasons for the next one

189
00:09:16,266 --> 00:09:17,933
You didn't miss out on a little something

190
00:09:17,933 --> 00:09:21,700
You just saved what's limited for places that still add value

191
00:09:21,833 --> 00:09:24,233
Next time we'll cover the equimarginal principle

192
00:09:24,466 --> 00:09:31,600
When money and time are both short how do you split them across choices without feeling you lost on every side
